    name: "Scaling actinium-225 supply with proton accelerators instead of the thorium-229 cow",
    tldr: "Most of the world's actinium-225, a rare radioactive metal used in some newer cancer treatments, comes from slowly extracting it from an old stockpile of another radioactive element. A different method — firing high-energy protons at ordinary thorium metal in an accelerator — can make much bigger batches, needed if actinium-225 treatments are ever to reach more than a small number of patients.",
    summary: "Historically, essentially all of the world's actinium-225 has come from 'milking' a legacy stockpile of thorium-229 (itself derived from uranium-233), a process that yields only about 1-1.2 curies a year worldwide, enough for roughly 1,000-1,200 patients and far too little to run more than the most preliminary clinical trials. The US Department of Energy's Isotope Program established the Ac-225 Tri-Lab Effort in 2014-2015 across Brookhaven, Los Alamos and Oak Ridge National Laboratories to develop an alternative: firing high-energy (greater than 70 MeV) proton beams at thorium-232 targets to produce actinium-225 by spallation, a reaction that does not depend on the finite thorium-229/uranium-233 stockpile.\n\nIn June 2021, Brookhaven demonstrated that this accelerator route could generate about 60% of the entire annual thorium-229-derived supply in a single 12-day irradiation and processing campaign. The FDA accepted a Type II Drug Master File for accelerator-produced actinium-225 nitrate from Oak Ridge National Laboratory in January 2020, and the Tri-Lab effort has since distributed more than 1,100 millicuries of accelerator-produced actinium-225 to evaluators, with batch sizes targeted to grow from the historical 50-100 mCi range toward 100-1,000 mCi per batch. Brookhaven completed an $8.5 million refurbishment of its All-Purpose Hot Cell facility in March 2023 specifically to expand actinium-225 processing capacity, and separately NorthStar Medical Radioisotopes has pursued a different accelerator route (electron-beam photonuclear transmutation of radium-226) intended to produce up to 200 curies a year per accelerator.\n\nA known trade-off of the proton-spallation route is co-production of actinium-227, a long-lived (22-year) alpha emitter; the DOE Isotope Program states this is kept to 2% or less of total activity at product release, and held a joint roundtable with the US Nuclear Regulatory Commission in March 2023 to work through the regulatory implications of that impurity for hospitals and clinics using the material.",
    hypothesis: "Producing actinium-225 by proton-beam spallation of thorium-232 in linear accelerators, rather than extracting it from the legacy thorium-229 stockpile, can scale world supply from roughly 1-1.2 curies a year to the hundreds-of-curies scale needed for large randomized trials and eventual commercial radioligand therapy.",
    rationale: "The DOE Tri-Lab effort demonstrated in 2021 that a single 12-day accelerator campaign could produce about 60% of the entire annual thorium-229-derived supply, and the FDA has already accepted a Drug Master File for the accelerator-produced material, showing the regulatory pathway is viable. The main open questions are whether batch sizes can be sustained at the 100-1,000 mCi scale needed for a registrational trial, and how regulators and clinics will handle the co-produced, long-lived actinium-227 impurity.",
    test: "The 2021 Brookhaven demonstration campaign already showed technical feasibility at scale; the next milestone is whether the renovated Brookhaven All-Purpose Hot Cell facility and expanded Los Alamos target capacity sustain routine batches in the hundreds of millicuries, and whether an actinium-225 radioligand therapy programme can complete a registrational trial supplied entirely from accelerator-produced material under an agreed actinium-227 impurity limit.",
    maturity: "being-tested-at-scale",
    actor: "engineering",
    cost: "large",
    horizonYears: 5,
    asOf: "2026-09-22",
